## 7. Limits, stated plainly

A blueprint published under a brand built on honesty owes you its limits in the same size type as its claims.

**The evidence, honestly.** The strongest supportable claim for one core piece of this method is narrow: if-then pre-commitments ("if situation X arises, I will do Y") measurably improve follow-through — a meta-analysis of 94 studies put the effect at d ≈ 0.65 (Gollwitzer & Sheeran, 2006). Relatedly, people who make scored predictions and receive prompt feedback on them improve their calibration modestly — on the order of a 10% improvement in standard forecast-accuracy scores in the Good Judgment Project research. Checklists reduce errors in bounded, repeatable work *when they are actually performed*. That is the evidentiary floor, and it is real but modest.

**What the evidence does not say.** A 2024 meta-analysis in *Nature Human Behaviour* (Vaccaro et al.), covering 106 experiments, found that human + AI combinations on average performed *worse* than the better of the two working alone (g ≈ −0.23), even though they beat humans working alone — with the losses concentrated in decision tasks, exactly the tasks this blueprint concerns. Anyone who tells you a system like this one guarantees a better-than-either-alone result is selling you something the literature does not support. This method's claim is different and deliberately smaller: pre-commitments, ledgers, and receipts make performance **measurable** and errors **convertible into rules**. A measurable pair can improve on evidence. An unmeasured pair can only improve on vibes — and the research on self-perception versus measured performance suggests vibes are where the largest, most confident errors live.

**What the method does not do.**

- It does not replace domain skill. A ledger will faithfully record a bad technician's bad calls and grade them WRONG with perfect accuracy. The hands leg of the upper hand is still earned in the world, not in the files.
- It does not make decisions for you. It makes your decisions *checkable*. The judgment — and the responsibility — remains where it belongs.
- It does not run itself. The upkeep is real: logging at the moment of the call, a weekly review, periodic drills. Budget roughly 20–30 minutes a week for a small system, and know that a system whose upkeep you resent will be abandoned by March — start at the starter kit's size, not the full architecture's.
- It does not fix a dishonest operator. Every mechanism here can be gamed by the person keeping the records — falsifiers written vaguely, grades softened, entries "forgotten." The method assumes the operator would rather be accurate than comfortable. If that assumption fails, the paperwork becomes theater, and no blueprint can save it.
- It is not a productivity multiplier, an income system, or a promise. Nothing in this document claims the method will make you money, save you a stated number of hours, or outperform anyone. It claims that your errors will get shorter lives and your decisions will leave evidence. What you build on that is your own result, and it will be graded by your own ledger.

